  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Request**\n\nI\'m now carefully dissecting the user\'s request, focusing on the core instruction: to build an app with specific screen designs. The user explicitly mentioned hotlinking images from HTML. I am also reviewing the attached TaskFlow Narrative design for the relevant visual context. I am ready to begin implementation as soon as I can confirm the necessary details.\n\n\n**Reconciling Design Specifications**\n\nI\'m now cross-referencing the TaskFlow Narrative design document with my frontend design rules. I\'m paying close attention to the specified fonts (Plus Jakarta Sans and JetBrains Mono) and the color palette, ensuring I adhere to the cool slate/blue tint background, white base canvas, and the specified primary, secondary, success, and destructive accents, along with the text hierarchy and borders.\n\n\n**Defining Responsive Layout**\n\nI\'m deep in the responsive design now, crafting the optimal layouts for desktop, tablet, and mobile. The focus is on a fixed sidebar on desktop, a collapsed rail on tablet, and a bottom navigation bar on mobile. I\'m prioritizing usability, with a maximum sidebar width on desktop, and a sticky bottom nav on mobile no wider than 15% of the viewport.\n\n\n**Implementing Task Views**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the task list\'s implementation, making sure task rows are independent cards with the specified framing. I\'m prioritizing the custom completion checkboxes with their animations. I\'m also including the strikethrough effect and considering options for inline subtasks, drag-and-drop reordering, and edit/delete features.\nI\'m also reminding myself of the front-end design rules for consistent, anti-slop design.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Guidelines**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the visual details. No pulsing dots, ever! I\'m planning to use a single-elevation depth with a subtle 1px border and a whisper ambient shadow. The goal is to avoid any nested card appearances. I also see a need for a real avatar image in the sidebar; I must create one with the `generate_image` function. I had to stop and check for the [AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START] metadata, which specified that image generation should only be used if explicitly requested. I see now that it\'s allowed!\n\n\n**Prioritizing Visual Consistency**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the empty state visuals, and the crisp inline SVG illustrations, with the specified colors, is looking like the best option. I plan to use a clean typographic monogram avatar, based on the user\'s initials, and avoid any image generation unless explicitly specified. I\'m taking the system instruction into consideration to avoid the generation of images.\n\n**Detailing Today Screen**\n\nI\'m now detailing the Today screen, `/today` view. I\'m focusing on the quick-add task bar and the segmented status filter. I\'m also planning the grouped sections and the expandable task inspector.\n\n\n**Planning Upcoming Screen**\n\nI\'m now outlining the `/upcoming` view, planning a chronological multi-day agenda with week-at-a-glance. I\'ll include sections like \'Today\', \'Tomorrow\', and \'Later This Week\'. I\'m also planning an interactive 7-day load bar to visualize daily task counts and allow for clean rescheduling.\n\n\n**Designing Weekly Review**\n\nI\'m now detailing the `/review` view. I\'m focusing on a calm, uncluttered productivity narrative with real stats: completion rate, tasks by project, priority distribution, and a 7-day completion bar chart (SVG/CSS). I\'m also planning the \"Weekly Reflection\" scratchpad. I\'m also planning a settings or preferences screen, accessed from the sidebar and its footer profile/preferences. This will include display name, default view, and several settings and export/reset options. Now, I will look at persistence and the type of storage to use. Since there\'s no auth, `localStorage` with sample data seems right.\n\n\n**Structuring Project Files**\n\nI\'m now outlining a modular file structure for the project. I\'ve updated `metadata.json` with the project name and description, and synced the title and description in `index.html`. Google Fonts for Plus Jakarta Sans and JetBrains Mono are now loaded. I\'m focusing on key directories like `/src` for styling with Tailwind and font setup, and clean TypeScript types and interfaces for crucial components.\n\n\n**Organizing Data and Components**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into implementation. I\'ve defined TypeScript types: `Priority`, `TaskStatus`, `ViewId`, and `UserPreferences`. I\'m also architecting a dynamic initial data set, and creating a robust custom hook for task management, which will provide project, preferences, search and undo functionality, as well as `localStorage` persistence. The sidebar will adjust to different form factors. I\'m also working on a quick-add input and task rows.\n\n\n**Implementing Task Actions**\n\nI\'m now implementing hover actions and active selection for task management, including edit, priority toggle, and delete utilities with a blue selection border. I\'m building out a clean slide-over drawer for task detail inspection and editing, with accessible modal fallback for smaller screens, while also designing components for empty states, project creation modals, the weekly review view, and upcoming view.\n\n\n**Reviewing Style Guide**\n\nI\'m now carefully reviewing the design tokens from the markdown and frontend design rules. I\'m ensuring consistency with the specified fonts, particularly Plus Jakarta Sans and JetBrains Mono. I\'m also confirming the canvas background, card, and border colors, as well as the text hierarchy and specific primary, secondary, and destructive colors. I am also planning the settings modal, and focusing on supporting both list and kanban board views.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Discipline**\n\nI\'m now integrating design tokens, focusing on metadata presentation in task rows; I\'ll use typographic separators for clarity. I\'m prioritizing clean filter buttons and avoiding decorative pulsing dots, implementing a subtle border for task selection. I\'m now planning the top bar layout and implementing the new task button.\n\n\n"
